{"id":"ridge-regression","t":"Ridge regression with an unpenalised intercept","tags":["Regression","Optimization"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"fit_ridge","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Ridge regression adds an L2 penalty on the weights, but **not** on the intercept:\n\n$$\\min_{w,\\,b}\\ \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(y_i - x_i^\\top w - b\\right)^2 + \\alpha \\lVert w\\rVert_2^2 .$$\n\nWrite `fit_ridge(X, y, alpha)` that returns the exact minimiser `(w, b)`.\n\n## Input\n- `X`: float array `(n, d)`; `y`: float array `(n,)`; `alpha >= 0`.\n- When `alpha == 0` the tests guarantee the centred design has full column rank. When `alpha > 0`, `d` may exceed `n` and columns may be collinear.\n\n## Output\n- `w`: float array `(d,)`; `b`: Python `float`.\n\n## Notes\n- The penalty is on the **sum** of squared errors (not the mean). Get the scaling right or your weights will differ.\n- Do not penalise `b`: shifting every target by a constant must shift `b` by that constant and leave `w` unchanged.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef fit_ridge(X, y, alpha):\n    \"\"\"Minimise ||y - Xw - b||^2 + alpha ||w||^2 (b unpenalised). Return (w (d,), b float).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Take the derivative with respect to `b` first. What does it tell you about where the fitted plane passes?","Setting $\\partial/\\partial b = 0$ gives $b = \\bar y - \\bar x^\\top w$. Substituting back leaves ridge on centred data with no intercept.","With $X_c = X - \\bar x$: `w = solve(Xc.T @ Xc + alpha * I, Xc.T @ (y - y.mean()))`, then `b = y.mean() - X.mean(0) @ w`."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef fit_ridge(X, y, alpha):\n    X, y = np.asarray(X, dtype=float), np.asarray(y, dtype=float)\n    x_mean, y_mean = X.mean(axis=0), y.mean()\n    Xc = X - x_mean                                   # centring removes b from the problem\n    A = Xc.T @ Xc + alpha * np.eye(X.shape[1])\n    w = np.linalg.solve(A, Xc.T @ (y - y_mean))\n    return w, float(y_mean - x_mean @ w)              # b puts the fitted plane through the means\n","ex_md":"## Why centring handles the intercept\n$\\partial/\\partial b = 0$ gives $b = \\bar y - \\bar x^\\top w$ for any $w$. Plugging it in turns the residual into $(y - \\bar y) - (X - \\bar x)w$, so the remaining problem is ridge with no intercept on centred data. Its gradient is $-2X_c^\\top(y_c - X_c w) + 2\\alpha w$; setting it to zero gives $(X_c^\\top X_c + \\alpha I)\\,w = X_c^\\top y_c$.\n\n## Why ridge always has a solution\n$X_c^\\top X_c$ is positive semidefinite, so adding $\\alpha I$ with $\\alpha > 0$ makes every eigenvalue at least $\\alpha$. The matrix is invertible even with collinear columns (test 3) or $d > n$ (test 7). In SVD terms, each component is shrunk by $s_j^2/(s_j^2 + \\alpha)$.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(nd^2 + d^3)$. When $d \\gg n$, use the dual form $w = X_c^\\top (X_cX_c^\\top + \\alpha I)^{-1} y_c$, which costs $O(n^2 d + n^3)$.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Appending a ones column and penalising it too. Then shifting `y` changes `w`, and the intercept shrinks toward 0.\n- Mixing up mean and sum scaling: $\\frac1n\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert^2 + \\alpha\\lVert w\\rVert^2$ is the same as using $n\\alpha$ here.\n- Using `np.linalg.inv` instead of `solve`.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Show that ridge is the MAP estimate under a Gaussian prior $w \\sim \\mathcal N(0, \\tau^2 I)$. What is $\\alpha$ in terms of the noise variance and $\\tau^2$?\n- Why should features be standardised before ridge? (The penalty treats all coefficients equally, so a feature measured in millimetres is penalised differently from the same feature in metres.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[0.],\n       [1.],\n       [2.],\n       [3.]])\narray([1., 3., 5., 7.])\n0.0","out":"(array([2.]), 1.0)"},{"in":"array([[0.],\n       [1.],\n       [2.],\n       [3.]])\narray([1., 3., 5., 7.])\n5.0","out":"(array([1.]), 2.5)"}],"lessons":[{"href":"../ml-maths-course/index.html#l08-03","t":"Linear Regression With Matrices","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"../ai-research-track-web/index.html#l02-04","t":"L1 vs L2 Norms","c":"AI Research"},{"href":"../machine-learning/index.html#l01-04","t":"Regularisation","c":"Machine Learning"},{"href":"../machine-learning/index.html#l02-02","t":"Ridge, lasso and elastic net","c":"Machine Learning"}],"related":["linear-regression","lasso-regression"],"ntests":8,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[0.0], [1.0], [2.0], [3.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [1.0, 3.0, 5.0, 7.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [2.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 1.0]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[0.0], [1.0], [2.0], [3.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [1.0, 3.0, 5.0, 7.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 5.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 2.5]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 4.0], [3.0, 6.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [1.0, 2.0, 2.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 1.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [0.09090909090909088, 0.18181818181818182], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.7575757575757577]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0], [2.0], [3.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [10.0, 20.0, 30.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 1000000000.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [1.999999996e-08], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 19.99999996]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[-1.738266398496882, -1.3366427931811324, -1.361106708564987, -0.35161713127840977], [-2.3125815796967033, -0.18889719608460778, -0.957229228096346, 0.8936001849299788], [0.956847237579234, 1.3922582291390866, 0.7674701130947078, -0.053029778757267734], [0.8597939889439096, 1.5054811563838433, -0.6535945334170485, 0.610351145830187], [-0.042673827710852374, 1.4400167254152394, -0.8368950200968434, -0.3015466095655266], [0.36233859316943917, 0.25811026702099754, -1.6394479624476481, 0.360155232237386], [-0.11849769951697288, -0.23974784920156203, -0.15530166200229314, 0.21897170507821917], [-1.8163956614546406, 1.5524665679968663, -0.8614416732885682, -2.2413678581872